\section{Matching is on the exact id string, so counts are lower bounds}{


Two individuals are treated as the same individual when their \code{id} strings
are identical. Where a dataset re-releases another project's individuals
under its own names, the link is missed. This is known to be material for
AADR against SGDP: 267 AADR individuals carry \code{S_}-prefixed ids following the
SGDP naming convention and \strong{none of them matches an SGDP id exactly},
against the 46 AADR-SGDP matches exact matching does find. The AADR \code{.anno}
source file carries an \verb{Individual ID} column, not currently shipped in
\code{sample_information.Rtable}, that resolves at least 95 of them
(\verb{S_Dusun-1.DG} has \verb{Individual ID = Dusun22}, which is an SGDP id in the
package).

\strong{The AADR-SGDP figure in particular is therefore a lower bound.} When that
column is added to the shipped table this function will gain a \code{match_basis}
column and the counts will rise; that is the fix landing, not a regression.
}

\section{How the filters interact with the overlap}{


\code{dataset} restricts the candidate pool \strong{before} overlap is computed, and is
the argument that asks a merge question. \code{dataset = c("HGDP", "SGDP")} asks
"which individuals are in both of these" and returns the 35 individuals those
two share, not the 929 that HGDP shares with the AADR. \code{dataset = NULL}
counts every dataset in the holdings, so \code{get_sample_overlap()} and
\code{get_sample_overlap(dataset = c("AADR", "KGP"))} are not the same question
restricted -- they are different questions.

\code{samples}, \code{pop} and \code{temporal} select which of the overlapping individuals
are \emph{reported}; they do not shrink the pool. An individual is kept when any
of its rows in the pool matches, and then all of its rows in the pool are
returned, so \code{n_datasets} and \code{datasets} keep describing the individual
rather than the filter. For \code{pop} this is the only workable reading:
population codes carry the dataset they came from, so every one of the 3,471
overlapping individuals has a \emph{different} \code{pop} in each dataset, and a \code{pop}
filter applied to the pool before the overlap was computed would always
return nothing.

Note that \code{temporal} describes the age of an individual's population, and the
AADR contributes several thousand ancient populations. Any analysis that
assumes a living group -- anything joined to ethnographic data through
\code{\link[=get_dplace_link]{get_dplace_link()}}, for instance -- wants \code{temporal = "modern"}.
}

\section{Within-dataset duplication is a different problem}{


An individual sequenced twice by one project, which the AADR records as two
library types (\code{.AG} and \code{.SG}) under one \code{id}, has \code{n_datasets == 1} and is
\strong{not} returned at the default \code{min_datasets = 2L}. That is deliberate:
within-dataset library duplication has a different fix, and folding it into
\code{n_datasets} would make the count uninterpretable. It is surfaced instead by
\code{n_source_ids}, which counts distinct \code{source_id} values for the individual
across the pool and is always returned.
}

